From: quickjs-ng object-creation work
Subject: Object creation: JSON.parse shape reuse (O3)

What this does
  Adds a per-nesting-depth expected-transition cache to the JSON parser. The
  first object at a depth records its key sequence (atoms) and the shape chain
  it produces (shapes[0..n]); the next object at the same depth that starts
  from the same base shape and repeats the same keys adopts each cached shape
  directly (json_obj_adopt_shape) instead of hashing every add-transition.
  A divergence drops the stale tail and rebuilds the chain; duplicate keys fall
  back to JS_DefinePropertyValue. The cache is freed by JS_ParseJSON_internal on
  both the success and the failure path, and is re-fetched after each recursive
  value parse because a nested object can grow the depth-indexed array.

Why we need it
  json_parse_value's object branch calls JS_DefinePropertyValue once per key,
  which goes through add_property's hashed shape lookup for every object. JSON
  documents repeat the same object layout, so the shape chain is identical from
  object to object and can be reused verbatim. On a 200-object x 100-iteration
  JSON.parse driver this drops shape allocations from 80,200 to 201 (-99.7%);
  the ProseMirror parse-only workload improves 2-8% (S 594->582us, M
  2.91->2.80ms, L 9.13->8.38ms).

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      JSONShapeCache + JSParseState json_shapes/json_shapes_len/
      json_shapes_cap; json_shape_cache_free / json_shape_cache_at /
      json_shape_cache_reserve; json_obj_adopt_shape; the depth parameter
      threaded through json_parse_value; the object-branch cache logic.

  Box2D checksum 546.1008391043414, test262 50/81192 with no leaks,
  differential corpus 398/0/0, ASan on test262-fast clean (one pre-existing
  UBSan allocator-vtable report). JSON.parse-only micro-benchmark (14
  correctness checks incl. duplicate keys, key reordering, __proto__ own
  property, reviver, deep nesting) identical to the pre-change engine.

